WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.733
Fine-tuning large language models normally requires
backpropagation,

00:00:04.813 --> 00:00:09.547
which stores activations and gradients and consumes
enormous memory.

00:00:09.627 --> 00:00:13.440
This paper, from Princeton University, introduces MeZO,

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:20.518
a memory-efficient zeroth-order optimizer that
fine-tunes language models using only forward passes.

00:00:20.598 --> 00:00:25.190
MeZO adapts the classical zeroth-order SGD method
to run in place,

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.517
so training costs the same memory as inference.

00:00:28.597 --> 00:00:34.038
On a single 80-gigabyte A100 GPU, MeZO can train a
30-billion-parameter model,

00:00:34.118 --> 00:00:38.285
where backpropagation with Adam fits only a
2.7-billion one.

00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:40.763
Across many tasks and model scales,

00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:45.930
MeZO matches fine-tuning performance with up to
twelve times less memory.
